Twist
=====

Copyright (C) 2026 Chris Burdess

Twist is a transfer client for the GNU system: a library, libtwist, and a
command-line program, twist.  The command line is mostly compatible with
curl's: the options, standard streams and exit status are the same, so that
twist can replace it in scripts.  The library has two objects, a transfer and
a runtime that runs transfers, and nothing in it blocks.

Schemes
-------

Each is a scheme of one transfer engine:

  http, https       HTTP/1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3
  ws, wss           WebSocket
  ftp, ftps         FTP
  sftp, scp         files over SSH (scp runs over SFTP)
  smb, smbs         files from a share, SMB 3.1.1 over TCP or over QUIC
  smtp, smtps       sending mail
  imap, imaps       fetching mail and listing mailboxes
  pop3, pop3s       retrieving mail
  ldap, ldaps       binding and searching, with the result as LDIF
  mqtt, mqtts       subscribing and publishing
  dict              DICT lookups
  gopher, gophers   Gopher
  rtsp              RTSP requests
  telnet            telnet sessions: the server's text out, standard input in
  tftp              TFTP
  file              local files

The schemes with an s added to the protocol's name (https, wss, ftps, smtps,
imaps, pop3s, ldaps, mqtts, gophers) are TLS from the first byte.  ftp, imap,
pop3, smtp and ldap can also be upgraded after they start (AUTH TLS, STARTTLS,
STLS and LDAP's StartTLS) with --ssl or --ssl-reqd; mqtt cannot.  smbs is SMB over QUIC,
not TLS on the SMB port.  rtmp and rtmps are recognised and not implemented;
see TODO.

TLS 1.2 and 1.3 and QUIC are quickstep's, and SSH is foxtrot's.  Transfers can
go through HTTP, HTTPS and SOCKS5 proxies (HTTP/3 through a CONNECT-UDP
proxy), and host names can be resolved over DNS-over-QUIC, -TLS and -HTTPS as
well as plain UDP.  The manual is doc/twist.texi ("make info"), and
doc/twist.1 is the short man page.

Dependencies
------------

  - quickstep, installed so that pkg-config can see quickstep.pc
  - foxtrot, the SSH engine, installed so that pkg-config can see foxtrot.pc
  - libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later, which foxtrot needs, and which the loader
    must find ahead of any older system copy (configure checks this)
  - zlib
  - libbrotli (libbrotlidec and libbrotlienc), optional, for the br content
    coding; without it twist asks for and decodes gzip and deflate only
  - GNU readline, for the twist program only

Build and install
-----------------

  ./bootstrap
  ./configure
  make
  make check
  make install

If quickstep is installed under a prefix pkg-config does not search,
pass that prefix:

  ./configure --with-quickstep=/prefix

The same applies to foxtrot, with --with-foxtrot=/prefix.

See INSTALL for the GNU build instructions, and TODO for what is
not yet implemented.

Performance
-----------

bench/ compares twist with curl on downloads and uploads over HTTP/1.1,
HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 against a local nginx, and reports wall time, CPU time and
memory (bench/run.sh runs it in a container; see HACKING for the options,
including the MTU of the loopback, which matters for HTTP/3).

For anything but a small request the two are close.  For a small one the
difference is start-up, and what makes it is how the two programs are linked,
not the code that runs.  The curl the benchmark uses is a single static binary.
Twist is dynamically linked, and loading its 22 shared libraries costs about
half a millisecond before it does anything, most of it the GnuTLS chain.  Linked
statically (everything but the C library, libm, libp11-kit and libffi, since
Debian has no static libp11-kit) twist takes 0.9 to 1.0 times curl's CPU for
small and medium requests, and large transfers are within about 15%, with a
large upload over HTTP/1.1, about 1.2 times, the one that is further.  Dynamic
against dynamic the picture is the same or better: on one test machine an
HTTPS request took 2.3 ms with twist and 3.2 ms with the distribution's curl
8.5.0.

These figures are from one machine (aarch64, in a container, over the
loopback) and will differ on others; the benchmark is there to be run.  The
build system makes the dynamic program.  A static one is made by linking the
objects of src/ with the static libraries of its dependencies, and its
licensing (the libraries are LGPL) is for whoever distributes it to consider.

